    // Part 2 — shokolad fabrikasi bo'ylab ekskursiya
    private static ListeningPart ListenT7P2() => new(2,
        "You will hear a guide talking to visitors at the start of a tour of a chocolate factory.",
        [
            F("Guide", "Good morning, everyone, and welcome to Hartley's Chocolate Works. My name's Carla, and I'll be taking you round the factory this morning. The tour lasts about an hour and a quarter, and before we go in, I'd like to tell you a little about the building and give you some practical information."),
            F("Guide", "Looking at the tall chimney and the big arched windows, most visitors guess that this building started life as a cotton mill. Others think it was a brewery, as there were plenty of those in this part of town. But in fact it was built in 1894 as a tram depot. The horse-drawn trams that carried people around the city were kept and repaired here, and the wide arches you walked through this morning are where the trams used to come in. The Hartley family bought the building in 1921 and turned it into a chocolate factory, and we've been here ever since."),
            F("Guide", "Now, cocoa only grows in hot, wet regions near the equator, so all our beans have to be imported. For most of the last century, the company bought its beans through large international traders, and they came from so many different farms that we never really knew where they had been grown. About ten years ago that changed. We now buy directly from small farming cooperatives in South America, mainly in Ecuador and Peru, and our buyers visit them every year."),
            F("Guide", "A few rules before we start. You're welcome to keep your bags with you, and you can take as many photos as you like. Our chocolate makers are quite used to it. But in the production area, everybody must wear a hairnet, even if you have very short hair. There's a box of them by the door. You won't need gloves, because you won't be touching any of the food."),
            F("Guide", "At the end of the tour, we'll have our tasting session. It's included in the price of your ticket, as it always has been, and it takes place in the tasting room, which is separate from the café. What's new this year is that, alongside our classic chocolates, we'll be offering samples from our new range, which is made without any milk at all, so it's suitable for vegans and for people who can't eat dairy."),
            F("Guide", "And please don't throw your ticket away. If you show it in the factory shop today, you'll get fifteen per cent off anything you buy, however much or little you spend. Sorry, but the discount doesn't work if you order from our website."),
            F("Guide", "Right, let me give you an idea of the route. We start in the Roasting Room, where the beans are roasted to bring out their flavour. It's extremely hot in there, so we don't actually go inside. Instead, you'll stand on a glass floor above the room and look straight down at the roasters. The roasting machines themselves are brand new, by the way. They were only installed last spring."),
            F("Guide", "From there we go to the Grinding Hall. This is where the roasted beans are crushed and ground into a thick paste. The huge stone grinders you'll see there were made in 1910, and amazingly, they're still in use every day. The noise is quite something."),
            F("Guide", "Next is the Moulding Line, where the liquid chocolate is poured into moulds. Here some of our chocolate makers will stop work for a few minutes to chat with you and answer your questions. Some of them have worked here for over thirty years, so they know everything there is to know. People often ask whether they can decorate their own bar here. We did offer that in the past, but we had to stop for hygiene reasons."),
            F("Guide", "Then comes the Wrapping Room, which is usually the children's favourite. There's a row of touch screens where you can design your own wrapper, choosing the colours and adding your name, and we'll print it and wrap a bar in it for you to take home."),
            F("Guide", "Our last stop before the tasting is the Heritage Gallery, which tells the story of the Hartley family. There are cases full of old wrappers and advertisements, and there's a short film, about ten minutes long, showing how the factory looked in the 1930s. You may have seen a game in our leaflet where you taste a sample and try to guess what's in it. I'm afraid that's only on our Saturday family tours. Right, if you've all got your hairnets, please follow me."),
        ],
        [
            new("mcq", Abc, null,
            [
                Mcq(11, "The factory building was originally", ["a cotton mill.", "a tram depot.", "a brewery."], "B",
                    "\"In fact it was built in 1894 as a tram depot\" — most visitors wrongly guess a cotton mill or a brewery."),
                Mcq(12, "Where does the factory get its cocoa beans now?", ["through large international traders", "from a single plantation", "directly from farmers' groups in South America"], "C",
                    "\"We now buy directly from small farming cooperatives in South America\" — buying through traders was the old system."),
                Mcq(13, "What must visitors do in the production area?", ["leave their bags outside", "wear a hairnet", "put on gloves"], "B",
                    "\"In the production area, everybody must wear a hairnet\"; bags are allowed and gloves are not needed."),
                Mcq(14, "What is new about the tasting session this year?", ["It is included in the ticket price.", "It takes place in the café.", "It includes chocolate made without milk."], "C",
                    "\"What's new this year is that ... our new range, which is made without any milk at all\"; it has always been included and is not in the café."),
                Mcq(15, "Visitors can get a discount in the factory shop if they", ["show their ticket.", "spend more than a certain amount.", "order online."], "A",
                    "\"If you show it in the factory shop today, you'll get fifteen per cent off\" — the amount spent does not matter and it doesn't work online."),
            ]),
            new("match", "What can visitors do in each area of the factory?\nChoose FIVE answers from the box and write the correct letter, A–G, next to Questions 16–20.", null,
            [
                Pick711(16, "Roasting Room", "F", "\"You'll stand on a glass floor above the room and look straight down at the roasters\" (the machines there are brand new, not old)."),
                Pick711(17, "Grinding Hall", "A", "\"The huge stone grinders ... were made in 1910, and ... they're still in use every day.\""),
                Pick711(18, "Moulding Line", "G", "\"Some of our chocolate makers will stop work for a few minutes to chat with you\" — decorating bars is no longer offered."),
                Pick711(19, "Wrapping Room", "C", "\"You can design your own wrapper, choosing the colours and adding your name.\""),
                Pick711(20, "Heritage Gallery", "B", "\"There's a short film, about ten minutes long\" — the guessing game is only on Saturday family tours."),
            ],
            [
                "see machinery that is more than a hundred years old",
                "watch a short film",
                "design their own wrapper",
                "decorate a chocolate bar",
                "guess the ingredients in a sample",
                "look down through a glass floor",
                "talk to the chocolate makers",
            ]),
        ]);

    // Part 3 — ikki talaba: qayta tiklanuvchi energiya haqidagi insho
    private static ListeningPart ListenT7P3() => new(3,
        "You will hear two students, Leila and Ben, discussing an essay they are writing together on renewable energy.",
        [
            F("Leila", "Hi, Ben. Have you got a few minutes to talk about the renewable energy essay? The deadline's only three weeks away."),
            M("Ben", "Sure. I've been reading Dr Patel's comments on our outline. She wasn't very keen on the idea of a general overview, was she?"),
            F("Leila", "No. She said there are already hundreds of essays comparing solar, wind and hydro power, and that she'd much rather we looked closely at one particular place. A case study, basically. She didn't mind which place we chose, or which kinds of energy we included, as long as we focused on a real example."),
            M("Ben", "So which place? I liked the idea of that Scottish island that runs mostly on wind power."),
            F("Leila", "Me too, but we'd be relying on whatever's been published about it, and there isn't much. What about our own campus? The university's estates department keeps data on the energy use of every building, and when I emailed them last week, they said they'd send us anything we need."),
            M("Ben", "That's a big advantage. I suppose it's not a completely new topic. There was a student project on it a few years ago. But that doesn't matter."),
            F("Leila", "Exactly. Being able to get hold of the figures easily is the main thing."),
            M("Ben", "Right. So, I've been looking at the solar panels on the library roof. According to the estates report, they're producing about thirty per cent less electricity than expected."),
            F("Leila", "Really? Why's that? I'd have guessed they were just dirty. There's been a lot of dust from all the building work."),
            M("Ben", "That's what I thought too, but they're cleaned twice a year, and the report says cleaning makes very little difference. And they were fitted at exactly the right angle. The problem is the new science block. It was built after the panels went up, and for most of the afternoon it casts a shadow over half of them."),
            F("Leila", "That's a useful point for the essay. Planning matters as much as the technology. What about wind? Was a turbine ever considered?"),
            M("Ben", "Yes, back in 2019. I assumed they'd dropped the idea because people living near the campus complained about the noise. There were a few objections, but that wasn't the reason. When they measured the wind on the site for a year, the average speed was simply too low for a turbine to be worth building."),
            F("Leila", "And what about the cost?"),
            M("Ben", "Money wasn't the issue. They'd already found funding for it."),
            F("Leila", "OK. Now, I'm a bit worried about our sources. We've got plenty of them, and most are recent, which is good."),
            M("Ben", "And they're not too technical, which is what I was afraid of."),
            F("Leila", "No, but have you noticed that nearly everything we've found comes from the university itself? Press releases, the sustainability web page, the annual report. They're bound to present things in a positive light."),
            M("Ben", "True. We need some independent sources as well, like newspaper reports or the government's energy statistics."),
            F("Leila", "So how shall we divide the work? Last time we each wrote half, and it didn't really read like one essay."),
            M("Ben", "What if we both draft some sections, and then swap and edit each other's work? That way the style should be more consistent."),
            F("Leila", "Good idea. Let's do that. So, let's sketch the plan. The introduction should explain why the university is doing all this."),
            M("Ben", "Yes, it has to mention the target. The university has promised to reach net zero by 2035, which means it will remove as much carbon as it produces."),
            F("Leila", "Then section two is the data. I think we should give the total electricity use for each year, but also the use per student, because student numbers have gone up so much. Otherwise the figures look worse than they really are."),
            M("Ben", "Agreed. And for section three, Dr Patel suggested comparing us with a similar university somewhere else. Didn't you find one in Norway?"),
            F("Leila", "I looked at it, but most of their electricity comes from hydropower, so it isn't really comparable. There's one in the Netherlands that's about the same size as ours and has a very similar climate. I think we should use that."),
            M("Ben", "Fine. And the conclusion should make a recommendation. Most of the buildings are still heated by gas boilers. I'd suggest replacing them with heat pumps, which could run on electricity from renewable sources."),
            F("Leila", "Good. That's our main recommendation, then: heat pumps. I'll start on the introduction tonight."),
        ],
        [
            new("mcq", Abc, null,
            [
                Mcq(21, "What did the tutor want the students to do in their essay?", ["compare different types of renewable energy", "make a detailed study of one location", "describe the history of renewable technology"], "B",
                    "\"She'd much rather we looked closely at one particular place. A case study, basically.\""),
                Mcq(22, "Why do they decide to write about their own campus?", ["The information they need is easy to obtain.", "Nobody has studied it before.", "The tutor suggested it."], "A",
                    "\"Being able to get hold of the figures easily is the main thing\"; a student project on it already exists."),
                Mcq(23, "Why do the solar panels on the library produce less electricity than expected?", ["They are not cleaned often enough.", "They were fitted at the wrong angle.", "A newer building blocks the sun."], "C",
                    "\"The problem is the new science block ... it casts a shadow over half of them.\""),
                Mcq(24, "According to the report, the plan for a wind turbine was dropped because", ["local residents complained about noise.", "there was not enough wind.", "it would have cost too much."], "B",
                    "\"The average speed was simply too low for a turbine to be worth building\"; noise \"wasn't the reason\" and money \"wasn't the issue\"."),
                Mcq(25, "What do the students see as the main weakness of their sources?", ["Many of them are out of date.", "They are too technical.", "Most were produced by the university."], "C",
                    "\"Nearly everything we've found comes from the university itself ... They're bound to present things in a positive light.\""),
                Mcq(26, "How will the students organise the writing?", ["Each will write half of the essay alone.", "One will do the research and the other will write.", "Each will edit sections written by the other."], "C",
                    "\"We both draft some sections, and then swap and edit each other's work.\""),
            ]),
            new("gap", NotesTwoWords711, 2,
            [
                Gap(27, "Introduction: the university's target to reach ___ by 2035", ["net zero"], "\"The university has promised to reach net zero by 2035.\""),
                Gap(28, "Section 2: show total electricity use and the use per ___", ["student"], "\"But also the use per student, because student numbers have gone up so much.\""),
                Gap(29, "Section 3: compare with a similar university in ___", ["Netherlands", "the Netherlands"], "\"There's one in the Netherlands ... I think we should use that\" (the Norwegian one is not comparable)."),
                Gap(30, "Conclusion: recommend replacing gas boilers with ___", ["heat pumps"], "\"I'd suggest replacing them with heat pumps.\""),
            ]),
        ]);

    // Part 4 — ma'ruza: hayvonlar migratsiyasi
    private static ListeningPart ListenT7P4() => new(4,
        "You will hear part of a university lecture about animal migration.",
        [
            M("Lecturer", "Good afternoon. Today's topic is animal migration: the regular, often very long journeys that many animals make between one place and another. I'll look at why animals migrate, how they find their way, how scientists study them, and some of the threats they face today."),
            M("Lecturer", "First, why migrate at all? Travelling long distances uses a great deal of energy, and it's often dangerous. The basic answer is that the benefits outweigh the costs. In most cases, animals are moving in search of food, and to find safe places to breed and raise their young. A bird that spends the summer in northern Europe, for example, can take advantage of the long days and huge numbers of insects, and then leave before the winter makes food impossible to find."),
            M("Lecturer", "The record holder among migrants is a small seabird, the Arctic tern. Each year it flies from its breeding grounds in the Arctic all the way to the Antarctic and back again. Tracking studies have shown that some individuals cover more than seventy thousand kilometres in a single year. And because it's summer at both ends of its journey, the Arctic tern probably sees more daylight than any other animal."),
            M("Lecturer", "So how do migrating animals find their way? It seems that most species combine several methods. Many birds use the position of the sun, correcting for the time of day. But a lot of small songbirds migrate at night. In a famous series of experiments in the 1960s, young birds were raised inside a planetarium, where the night sky could be controlled. When the artificial sky was rotated, the birds changed direction to match it, which showed that they were using patterns of stars to steer by."),
            M("Lecturer", "A second important ability is sensing the Earth's magnetic field. This has been demonstrated in birds, and also in sea turtles, which can return to the same beach where they hatched after many years at sea. Exactly how they detect it is still being debated."),
            M("Lecturer", "Salmon give us a third example. After several years in the ocean, they return to breed in the river where they were born. Near the coast they probably use magnetic information too, but to find the right river, and even the right stream, they rely on smell. Each stream has its own chemical signature, which the young fish remember."),
            M("Lecturer", "Insects migrate too. The monarch butterfly of North America flies several thousand kilometres south to spend the winter in the mountains of central Mexico. What's remarkable is that no single butterfly makes the whole round trip. The return journey north in spring is completed by several generations, each living for only a few weeks, laying eggs and dying along the way."),
            M("Lecturer", "On land, perhaps the best-known migration is that of the wildebeest of East Africa. More than a million of them move in a great circle through Tanzania and Kenya, and their timing isn't fixed by the calendar. Instead, they follow the rains, which bring fresh grass."),
            M("Lecturer", "Now, how do we actually study these journeys? The oldest method is ringing. In 1899, a Danish teacher called Hans Mortensen fitted small metal rings to the legs of starlings. When some of these birds were later found far away, he could work out where they had gone. Ringing is still used, but it depends on birds being found again, which rarely happens."),
            M("Lecturer", "Today, larger animals can carry GPS tags, which send their position by satellite. Small birds can't carry heavy equipment, so for them scientists use tiny devices called geolocators. These don't transmit anything. They simply record light levels, and from the times of sunrise and sunset, researchers can calculate roughly where the bird was each day. Of course, the bird has to be caught again so that the data can be downloaded."),
            M("Lecturer", "Finally, the threats. Many migrants depend on a small number of stopover sites where they rest and feed, and when these are drained or built on, whole populations suffer. Climate change is creating a different problem, which is a mismatch in timing. In parts of Europe, warmer springs mean that caterpillars now appear earlier than they used to. But some birds still arrive from Africa at the traditional time, and by then the peak supply of caterpillars, the food their chicks depend on, has already passed. Next week, we'll look at what conservationists are doing to protect migratory species across international borders."),
        ],
        [
            new("gap", NotesOneWord711, 1,
            [
                Gap(31, "Main reasons for migration: to search for ___ and safe places to breed", ["food"], "\"Animals are moving in search of food, and to find safe places to breed.\""),
                Gap(32, "Arctic tern: flies each year between the Arctic and the ___", ["Antarctic"], "\"It flies from its breeding grounds in the Arctic all the way to the Antarctic and back again.\""),
                Gap(33, "Night-flying songbirds steer using patterns of ___", ["stars"], "\"They were using patterns of stars to steer by.\""),
                Gap(34, "Birds and sea turtles can sense the Earth's ___ field", ["magnetic"], "\"Sensing the Earth's magnetic field ... in birds, and also in sea turtles.\""),
                Gap(35, "Salmon find the right river by ___", ["smell"], "\"To find the right river ... they rely on smell.\""),
                Gap(36, "Monarch butterflies: the journey north is completed by several ___", ["generations"], "\"The return journey north in spring is completed by several generations.\""),
                Gap(37, "Wildebeest: follow the ___, which bring fresh grass", ["rains"], "\"They follow the rains, which bring fresh grass.\""),
                Gap(38, "Ringing: first used in 1899 by a Danish ___", ["teacher"], "\"In 1899, a Danish teacher called Hans Mortensen fitted small metal rings.\""),
                Gap(39, "Geolocators: record ___ levels to estimate a bird's position", ["light"], "\"They simply record light levels.\""),
                Gap(40, "Climate change: some birds arrive after the peak supply of ___", ["caterpillars"], "\"By then the peak supply of caterpillars ... has already passed.\""),
            ]),
        ]);

