    // Part 4 — ma'ruza: rang fizikasi
    private static ListeningPart ListenT8P4() => new(4,
        "You will hear part of a university lecture about the science of colour.",
        [
            F("Lecturer", "Good morning, everyone. Today we're going to look at colour: what it actually is, how we see it, and why different animals, and even different languages, divide up the world of colour in different ways."),
            F("Lecturer", "Let's start with the physics. In the 1660s, Isaac Newton let a narrow beam of sunlight into a darkened room and passed it through a prism, a triangular block of glass. The white light spread out into a band of colours, from red to violet. Other people had seen this effect before, but they believed that the glass was somehow adding the colour. Newton showed they were wrong by passing a single colour through a second prism. It stayed the same colour. White light, he concluded, is a mixture of all the colours."),
            F("Lecturer", "So why does an apple look red? When white light falls on an object, the object absorbs some wavelengths and reflects others. A red apple absorbs most of the light that falls on it, but it reflects red light, so red is what reaches our eyes. In other words, what we call the colour of an object is really the light it reflects."),
            F("Lecturer", "Now to the eye. At the back of the eye, the retina contains two main types of light-sensitive cells. Rod cells work well in dim light, but they can't tell colours apart, which is why everything looks grey on a dark night. Colour vision depends on cone cells. Most people have three kinds of cone, which respond best to short, medium and long wavelengths, roughly blue, green and red. The brain compares the signals from the three types to produce every colour we see."),
            F("Lecturer", "If one type of cone doesn't work normally, the result is colour blindness. The most common form makes it hard to tell red from green. Because of the way it's inherited, it's far more common in men. About one in twelve men is affected, compared with around one in two hundred women."),
            F("Lecturer", "Other animals see the world very differently. Many birds and insects can see ultraviolet light, which is invisible to us. Bees are a good example. Many flowers that look plain to us have ultraviolet patterns on their petals, often lines or rings pointing towards the centre. These work rather like the lights on an airport runway, guiding the bee towards the nectar."),
            F("Lecturer", "Not all colour in nature comes from pigments, however. Some of the most brilliant colours are what we call structural colours. The wings of the blue morpho butterfly of South America contain no blue pigment at all. Instead, their scales are covered in microscopic ridges, which are arranged so that they reflect blue light very strongly while other colours cancel out. Peacock feathers work in a similar way. One interesting consequence is that structural colours don't fade. Pigments gradually break down in sunlight, but a morpho wing in a museum can look as bright after a century as on the day it was collected."),
            F("Lecturer", "Finally, let's consider language. Languages differ in how many basic colour words they have. Some use a single word for both green and blue. In others, the division is finer than in English. Russian, for example, has two separate basic words for blue: goluboy for light blue and siniy for dark blue. In one well-known study, Russian and English speakers were shown three squares of blue and asked which of two squares matched the third. The Russian speakers were faster at telling the shades apart when they fell on different sides of the boundary between their two words. This suggests that the language we speak can have a small but measurable effect on how we perceive colour. Next time, we'll look at how colour is used in art and design."),
        ],
        [
            new("gap", NotesOneWord711, 1,
            [
                Gap(31, "Newton split white light into colours using a ___", ["prism"], "\"Passed it through a prism, a triangular block of glass.\""),
                Gap(32, "The colour of an object is the light that it ___", ["reflects"], "\"What we call the colour of an object is really the light it reflects.\""),
                Gap(33, "Colour vision depends on ___ cells, of which there are three kinds", ["cone", "cones"], "\"Colour vision depends on cone cells. Most people have three kinds of cone\" (rods cannot tell colours apart)."),
                Gap(34, "Red-green colour blindness affects about 1 in 12 ___", ["men"], "\"About one in twelve men is affected, compared with around one in two hundred women.\""),
                Gap(35, "Bees can see ___ light, which humans cannot see", ["ultraviolet"], "\"Many birds and insects can see ultraviolet light, which is invisible to us.\""),
                Gap(36, "Patterns on petals guide bees to the ___", ["nectar"], "\"Guiding the bee towards the nectar.\""),
                Gap(37, "Blue morpho butterfly: scales covered in microscopic ___", ["ridges"], "\"Their scales are covered in microscopic ridges.\""),
                Gap(38, "Unlike pigments, structural colours do not ___", ["fade"], "\"Structural colours don't fade.\""),
                Gap(39, "Russian has two basic words for ___", ["blue"], "\"Russian ... has two separate basic words for blue.\""),
                Gap(40, "Russian speakers were ___ at telling the shades apart", ["faster"], "\"The Russian speakers were faster at telling the shades apart.\""),
            ]),
        ]);

