    // Part 4 — ma'ruza: qadimiy yer osti sug'orish kanallari (qanot/korez)
    private static ListeningPart ListenT9P4() => new(4,
        "You will hear part of a university lecture about ancient underground water channels known as qanats.",
        [
            M("Lecturer", "Good morning. In today's lecture on water management in dry regions, I'm going to talk about one of the most remarkable engineering achievements of the ancient world: the qanat. It has different names in different countries. In Afghanistan and parts of Central Asia, including Uzbekistan, it's usually called a karez. In Oman it's known as a falaj, and in North Africa as a foggara. But the basic principle is the same everywhere."),
            M("Lecturer", "A qanat is a gently sloping underground tunnel that carries water from an underground source, usually at the foot of hills or mountains, to fields and villages some distance away. What makes it so remarkable is that it needs no pumps and no energy of any kind. The water flows by gravity alone, from higher ground to lower ground, day and night."),
            M("Lecturer", "Where did the technology begin? Most historians believe that qanats were first built in ancient Persia, in what is now Iran, around three thousand years ago. From there, the idea spread along trade routes, east to Central Asia and western China, and west to the Arabian Peninsula, North Africa and eventually Spain."),
            M("Lecturer", "So how was a qanat built? The first step was to find water. The builders would dig a test shaft near the foot of the hills, called the mother well, and keep digging until they reached the water table. This could be many tens of metres below the surface."),
            M("Lecturer", "Then, starting from the point where the water was to come out, at a lower level, they dug a tunnel back towards the mother well, rising very slightly all the way. At regular intervals, perhaps every twenty or thirty metres, they dug vertical shafts down to the tunnel. These shafts had two purposes. They were used to lift out the soil that had been dug, and they allowed air to reach the workers underground. From the air, a line of qanat shafts looks like a row of small craters across the desert."),
            M("Lecturer", "Getting the slope right was critical. If the tunnel was too steep, the water would flow too fast and cause erosion, gradually wearing away the walls and floor of the tunnel until it collapsed. But if it was too flat, the water wouldn't flow at all."),
            M("Lecturer", "The men who did this work were highly skilled and highly respected. The job was dangerous, and the knowledge was usually passed down within families, with sons learning the trade from their fathers."),
            M("Lecturer", "Why go to all this trouble, instead of simply digging an open canal? The main advantage is that the water travels underground. In a hot, dry climate, water in an open channel is quickly lost through evaporation, but in a qanat very little is lost in this way, and the water arrives clean and cool."),
            M("Lecturer", "Qanats also shaped the way communities were organised. Because water was so precious, a fair system of sharing was essential. Each farmer had the right to the water for a certain length of time. In many Iranian villages, time was measured with a simple water clock: a small metal bowl with a hole in the bottom, floating in a larger container of water. When the bowl filled up and sank, one unit of time had passed, and the person in charge would note it down and start again."),
            M("Lecturer", "In towns, qanats were also used to cool buildings. Air cooled by the flowing water could be drawn up into houses through tall towers called windcatchers, and this natural air conditioning helped people to cool their homes in the hottest months."),
            M("Lecturer", "Today, the future of many qanats is uncertain. The most serious threat comes from modern wells with powerful electric pumps. These can take water out of the ground much faster than it's replaced, which lowers the water table, so that the qanats simply run dry. Another problem is that as young people move to the cities, there are fewer people left to carry out the regular maintenance. There is some good news, however. In 2016, a group of Persian qanats was added to the UNESCO World Heritage List, and in several countries local communities are now working to restore them."),
        ],
        [
            new("gap", NotesOneWord711, 1,
            [
                Gap(31, "A qanat carries water using only ___", ["gravity"], "\"The water flows by gravity alone.\""),
                Gap(32, "Probably first built in ancient ___ about 3,000 years ago", ["Persia"], "\"Qanats were first built in ancient Persia, in what is now Iran.\""),
                Gap(33, "Construction began with a test shaft called the mother ___", ["well"], "\"A test shaft near the foot of the hills, called the mother well.\""),
                Gap(34, "Vertical shafts: used to remove soil and to let ___ reach the workers", ["air"], "\"They allowed air to reach the workers underground.\""),
                Gap(35, "A tunnel that was too steep could suffer from ___", ["erosion"], "\"The water would flow too fast and cause erosion.\""),
                Gap(36, "Diggers usually learnt the trade from their ___", ["fathers"], "\"With sons learning the trade from their fathers.\""),
                Gap(37, "Main advantage: little water is lost through ___", ["evaporation"], "\"In a qanat very little is lost in this way\" — i.e. through evaporation."),
                Gap(38, "Water clock: a small metal ___ that sank when full", ["bowl"], "\"A small metal bowl with a hole in the bottom ... When the bowl filled up and sank.\""),
                Gap(39, "Used with windcatchers to ___ houses", ["cool"], "\"This natural air conditioning helped people to cool their homes.\""),
                Gap(40, "Main threat: modern wells with electric ___", ["pumps"], "\"Modern wells with powerful electric pumps ... lowers the water table.\""),
            ]),
        ]);

