    // Part 3 — o'qituvchining dissertatsiya bo'yicha fikri (ijtimoiy tarmoqlar va o'smirlar)
    private static ListeningPart ListenT10P3() => new(3,
        "You will hear a student, Grace, receiving feedback from her tutor, Dr Holt, on her dissertation about social media and teenagers.",
        [
            M("Dr Holt", "Hello, Grace, come in. I've read the full draft of your dissertation, so let's go through it together."),
            F("Grace", "Thanks. I have to admit I'm a bit nervous."),
            M("Dr Holt", "There's no need. There's a lot of good work here. Let me first check that I've understood your focus. Most research on teenagers and social media looks at the negative effects, on sleep, for example, or on anxiety. But you've looked at something rather different."),
            F("Grace", "Yes. I wanted to find out how teenagers actually use social media to maintain their friendships, especially with people they don't see at school every day. I did think about looking at sleep, or at school results, but there's already so much research on those."),
            M("Dr Holt", "And remind me where you collected your data."),
            F("Grace", "I originally planned to go to a youth club, but they couldn't guarantee who would turn up each week. In the end, two secondary schools agreed to take part, and I worked with students aged fourteen to sixteen."),
            M("Dr Holt", "You used focus groups rather than one-to-one interviews. Why was that?"),
            F("Grace", "People assume it's to save time, but actually organising the groups took ages. The main reason was that the teachers told me teenagers are much more relaxed talking in a group with their friends than alone with an adult they don't know. And that was my experience too. They talked quite openly."),
            M("Dr Holt", "That's a good justification. Make sure it's clearly explained. Now, the title. At the moment it's 'Social media and teenage friendship'. The problem is that it doesn't tell the reader who you actually studied. I'd add the age group, fourteen- to sixteen-year-olds."),
            F("Grace", "Not make it shorter?"),
            M("Dr Holt", "No, the length is fine, and once you add the age range it won't be too broad either. Next, the overall length. You're about fifteen hundred words over the limit."),
            F("Grace", "I know. I was going to ask whether I could cut the literature review."),
            M("Dr Holt", "I'd rather you didn't. What's taking up the space is the long quotations from your focus groups. Keep the best short quotations in the text, and move the longer extracts to an appendix. Appendices don't count towards the word limit."),
            F("Grace", "OK, that's easy enough."),
            M("Dr Holt", "Right, now some comments chapter by chapter. The introduction is well written, and the length is about right. But it never actually says what you're trying to find out. So open it with a clear research question in the first paragraph."),
            F("Grace", "OK. And the literature review? Is it too short?"),
            M("Dr Holt", "The length is fine. The issue is that many of your sources were published before 2015. The apps teenagers use have changed completely since then, so you need more up-to-date studies."),
            F("Grace", "Right. What about the methodology?"),
            M("Dr Holt", "Generally sound. But you don't explain how the students in your focus groups were chosen. Did the teachers pick them? Did they volunteer? The reader needs to know, because it affects how we interpret the results."),
            F("Grace", "The teachers asked for volunteers. I'll add that."),
            M("Dr Holt", "Now, the findings. It isn't your strongest chapter yet, but it could be, because the content is very interesting. The problem is that there are pages of numbers written out in sentences, like how many students used each app and how often. It's hard to follow. Put those figures into charts or tables."),
            F("Grace", "And the conclusion?"),
            M("Dr Holt", "Here I have a bigger concern. You say that social media improves teenagers' friendships. But your data only shows what teenagers told you about their own experiences. You can't claim that social media causes anything. Say what your data shows, and no more."),
            F("Grace", "So I need to be more careful with the wording."),
            M("Dr Holt", "Exactly. But overall, you should be pleased. It's a solid piece of work."),
        ],
        [
            new("mcq", Abc, null,
            [
                Mcq(21, "What is the main focus of Grace's research?", ["the effect of social media on sleep", "how teenagers use social media to keep up friendships", "the link between social media and school results"], "B",
                    "\"I wanted to find out how teenagers actually use social media to maintain their friendships.\""),
                Mcq(22, "Where did Grace collect her data?", ["at a youth club", "in two schools", "through an online survey"], "B",
