    // Part 4 — ma'ruza: vulqonlar
    private static ListeningPart ListenT10P4() => new(4,
        "You will hear part of a university lecture about volcanoes.",
        [
            F("Lecturer", "Good afternoon. Today we're turning to one of the most powerful forces on our planet: volcanoes. I'll explain where they occur, why some erupt gently and others explosively, and how scientists try to predict eruptions."),
            F("Lecturer", "First, the word itself. 'Volcano' comes from Vulcan, the Roman god of fire. There's also a small island near Sicily called Vulcano, which the Romans believed was the entrance to Vulcan's workshop."),
            F("Lecturer", "So where are volcanoes found? The great majority are located along the boundaries of the tectonic plates, the huge pieces into which the Earth's outer layer is broken. Around three quarters of the world's active volcanoes lie around the edges of the Pacific Ocean, in what's known as the Ring of Fire, which stretches from New Zealand through Japan and Alaska and down the west coast of the Americas to Chile."),
            F("Lecturer", "But there are exceptions. The Hawaiian Islands are in the middle of the Pacific plate, thousands of kilometres from the nearest boundary. They've formed above what's called a hotspot, a column of unusually hot rock rising from deep inside the Earth. As the plate moves slowly over the hotspot, new volcanoes form, while the older ones are carried away and become extinct. That's why the islands form a long chain."),
            F("Lecturer", "Now, why do some volcanoes erupt gently, while others explode? The key is the magma, the molten rock beneath the surface, and in particular the amount of silica it contains. Magma that's low in silica is runny, so gas escapes from it easily, and the lava simply pours out and flows away. Magma with a lot of silica is thick and sticky. The gas can't escape, pressure builds up, and eventually the volcano explodes."),
            F("Lecturer", "These differences also produce different shapes. Runny lava can travel a long way before it cools, building shield volcanoes, which are very wide with gentle slopes. Mauna Loa in Hawaii is the classic example. Thicker lava builds steep, cone-shaped volcanoes made of layers of lava and ash, like Mount Fuji in Japan."),
            F("Lecturer", "The most dangerous feature of an explosive eruption usually isn't the lava, which generally moves slowly enough for people to get out of the way. It's the pyroclastic flow, an avalanche of hot gas, ash and rock that races down the side of the volcano. These flows can travel at more than a hundred kilometres per hour, and it was a pyroclastic flow that buried the Roman town of Pompeii in the year 79."),
            F("Lecturer", "Large eruptions can also affect the climate. When Mount Tambora in Indonesia erupted in 1815, it threw so much ash and gas into the atmosphere that it partly blocked the sunlight. The following year, 1816, became known in Europe and North America as the year without a summer, with snow in June and crop failures in many areas."),
            F("Lecturer", "To compare eruptions, volcanologists use the Volcanic Explosivity Index, which runs from zero to eight. It's based mainly on the amount of material thrown out, and each step up the scale represents an eruption roughly ten times larger than the one before."),
            F("Lecturer", "So can we predict eruptions? Not precisely, but there are usually warning signs. Seismometers record the small earthquakes that happen as magma moves upwards. Scientists also measure the gases coming out of the volcano, and they use GPS and satellite images to detect swelling of the ground as magma collects underneath it."),
            F("Lecturer", "Finally, volcanoes aren't only destructive. Volcanic ash breaks down into some of the most fertile soils in the world, which is why farmers have lived on the slopes of volcanoes for thousands of years. And in Iceland, geothermal energy, that is, heat from underground, is used to heat around ninety per cent of homes. Next week, we'll look at earthquakes."),
        ],
        [
            new("gap", NotesOneWord711, 1,
            [
                Gap(31, "The word 'volcano' comes from Vulcan, the Roman ___ of fire", ["god"], "\"'Volcano' comes from Vulcan, the Roman god of fire.\""),
                Gap(32, "About three quarters of active volcanoes lie around the ___ Ocean", ["Pacific"], "\"Around three quarters ... lie around the edges of the Pacific Ocean.\""),
                Gap(33, "Hawaii: the islands formed above a ___", ["hotspot"], "\"They've formed above what's called a hotspot.\""),
                Gap(34, "Magma containing a lot of ___ is thick and traps gas", ["silica"], "\"Magma with a lot of silica is thick and sticky. The gas can't escape.\""),
                Gap(35, "Shield volcanoes: very wide, with gentle ___", ["slopes"], "\"Shield volcanoes, which are very wide with gentle slopes.\""),
                Gap(36, "Pyroclastic flows can travel at more than ___ km per hour", ["100", "hundred"], "\"These flows can travel at more than a hundred kilometres per hour.\""),
                Gap(37, "Tambora: 1816 was called the year without a ___", ["summer"], "\"1816, became known ... as the year without a summer.\""),
                Gap(38, "Explosivity Index: each step means an eruption about ___ times larger", ["ten", "10"], "\"Each step up the scale represents an eruption roughly ten times larger.\""),
                Gap(39, "Warning signs: GPS and satellites detect ___ of the ground", ["swelling"], "\"To detect swelling of the ground as magma collects underneath it.\""),
                Gap(40, "Iceland: ___ energy heats around 90% of homes", ["geothermal"], "\"In Iceland, geothermal energy ... is used to heat around ninety per cent of homes.\""),
            ]),
        ]);

