            M("Lecturer", "Good morning. Today's lecture is about the history of chess, one of the oldest board games still played today, and one which has changed considerably over the centuries."),
            M("Lecturer", "Most historians agree that chess originated in northern India around the sixth century. The early game was called chaturanga, a Sanskrit word meaning 'four divisions', and the pieces represented the four divisions of an army of that period."),
            M("Lecturer", "Those divisions were the infantry, which became our pawns; the cavalry, which became the knights; the elephants, which eventually turned into the bishops; and the chariots, which became the rooks, or castles."),
            M("Lecturer", "From India, the game travelled to Persia, where it was known as shatranj, and many chess terms come from Persian. The word 'check' comes from 'shah', meaning king, and 'checkmate' comes from the Persian phrase 'shah mat'. This is often translated as 'the king is dead', but a more accurate translation is 'the king is helpless'. In chess, of course, the king is never actually captured."),
            M("Lecturer", "The game then spread across the Arab world, where strong players wrote the first books analysing chess positions, and from there it reached Europe. By around the year 1000 it was being played in Spain and in Italy, and within two centuries it was known across most of the continent."),
            M("Lecturer", "However, medieval chess was a much slower game than the one we play now. The piece that stood next to the king, which later became our queen, was one of the weakest on the board. It could only move one square at a time, diagonally. The bishop, too, was limited to a short jump of two squares."),
            M("Lecturer", "This changed dramatically at the end of the fifteenth century, probably in Spain. The queen was given the power to move any distance in any direction, making her the most powerful piece on the board, and the bishop also gained its modern long-range move. As a result, games became much faster, and attacks could be launched very quickly."),
            M("Lecturer", "The modern competitive era began in the nineteenth century. In 1851, London hosted the first international chess tournament, organised at the same time as the Great Exhibition. It was won by Adolf Anderssen, a German mathematics teacher."),
            M("Lecturer", "Around this time, there was growing frustration with players who spent hours thinking about a single move. To solve this, time limits were introduced. At first, sand glasses, rather like large egg timers, were used to measure the time, but these were soon replaced by mechanical clocks with two faces, one for each player."),
            M("Lecturer", "In 1924, the international chess federation, known by its French initials FIDE, was founded in Paris, and it still organises the world championship today."),
            M("Lecturer", "The twentieth century also brought computers. In 1997, a computer called Deep Blue, built by IBM, defeated the world champion Garry Kasparov in a match, something many experts had thought impossible. Today, free chess programs on a phone can beat any human player, and professionals use them to prepare and train."),
            M("Lecturer", "Chess has also become more international than ever. In 2022, a very young team from Uzbekistan won the gold medal in the open section of the Chess Olympiad, ahead of far more experienced teams, a result that has inspired many young players across Central Asia. Next week, we'll look at the psychology of expert chess players."),
        ],
